<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jiménez dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/905">905</a>–<a href="/years/1234">1234</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Jimena dynasty</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Jiménez dynasty ruled the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Pamplona</a>, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a>, from 905, when Sancho Garcés I took the throne, until 1234. Its greatest king, Sancho III, dominated Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in the early 11th century, and his sons founded the royal lines of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and Aragon. After the death of Sancho VII in 1234, Navarre passed to the counts of Champagne.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jimenez-dynasty.jpg" alt="Jiménez dynasty" width="320" height="384" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Armas_de_la_Dinastia_Jimena_de_Navarra.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The family was also called the Jimena, the Sancha, the Banu Sancho, the Abarca or the Banu Abarca. Its first known member, García Jiménez of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Pamplona</a>, is obscure: the Códice de Roda calls him king of another part of the kingdom of Pamplona, probably the frontier areas of Álava and the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a> beyond the direct rule of the Íñiguez kings. It was long believed that the family came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a>. His younger son Sancho Garcés used foreign help to displace the Íñiguez ruler Fortún Garcés.</p><p>Muslim sources called the family the Banu Sanjo after Sancho, while the chronicler Ibn al-Kardabūs named Sancho III ibn Abarca, after a nickname of Sancho I. Sancho I repelled several attacks from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, crushed the neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-qasi">Banu Qasi</a>, extended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Pamplona</a> to the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a> valley and took in the County of Aragon.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After Sancho I's death in 925 his brother Jimeno Garcés held power, and then Sancho's young son García Sánchez I ruled under the guidance of his mother, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toda-of-pamplona">Toda Aznárez</a>, who built alliances among the Christian states and in 934 pledged allegiance to her nephew Abd-ar-Rahman III. Three generations of defeat and subjection by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliphate</a> followed, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sancho-iii-of-navarre">Sancho the Great</a>, who ruled from 1000 to 1035, freed the kingdom and also ruled Aragon, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ribagorza">Ribagorza</a> and eventually León.</p><p>His lands were divided among his sons. Ferdinand I killed the King of León and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> in 1037 and inherited his realms, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>. In 1076 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sancho-iv-of-navarre">Sancho IV of Navarre</a> was assassinated by his siblings, and his kingdom was shared between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and Aragon. Alfonso the Battler briefly reunited the family's lands through his marriage to Urraca, Queen of Castile and León, but the marriage failed. Aragon passed through the heiress Petronilla to the ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> through Blanca, sister of Sancho VII.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In the 15th century the Borgias of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> claimed descent from Pedro de Atarés, lord of Borja, a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sancho-ramirez">Sancho Ramírez</a>, Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ribagorza">Ribagorza</a>, which would have made them male-line descendants of the dynasty. The pedigree was a fabrication.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jiménez dynasty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 905 a rival family took power, and Sancho Garcés I, founder of the <b>Jimena dynasty</b>, broke with the Banu Qasi and expanded southward.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-navarre">Kingdom of Navarre · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
